/****************************************************************************
 * RK3576 Gigabit Ethernet MAC (DWC EQOS) driver.
 *
 * Four layers, bottom up, each readable on its own:
 *
 *   platform   the CRU clocks, the SDGMAC_GRF RGMII mode, the VCCIO delay
 *              lines and the controller reset.  Every write here fails
 *              silently if it is wrong -- a wrong gate bit or a wrong GRF
 *              offset produces no fault, only a controller that reads zero --
 *              so each one is read back and logged.
 *   PHY        MDIO over the controller's own bus, clause 22, and the link
 *              state machine.  The part is described, not coded around:
 *              struct phy_desc_s (<nuttx/net/mii.h>) carries its identity, the
 *              address range to look in, and -- for parts that have one -- how
 *              to decode its own status register.
 *   data       one descriptor ring each way, the interrupt, and the
 *              ownership rules that go with them.
 *   netdev     the NuttX lower half (netdev_lowerhalf), which owns RX
 *              deferral, TX polling, quota, carrier and statistics.
 *
 * The PHY code is in this file on purpose: it is the shape every NuttX MAC
 * driver has (imx9_enet, imxrt_enet, stm32_ethernet, sam_emac, ..., all of
 * them), because the management bus belongs to the MAC and clause 22 is the
 * same for every part.  What differs between parts arrives as data, in the
 * descriptor the board supplies, exactly as imx9's BOARD_ENETn_PHY_LIST does.
 * NuttX has no PHY driver layer to hang a separate driver on: <nuttx/net/phy.h>
 * is the SIGIO link-notification service and nothing else.
 *
 * The two instances share nothing but the CRU gate register (different bits)
 * and the clock tree; the MDIO buses are separate, so the two PHYs may -- and
 * do -- answer at the same address.
 ****************************************************************************/

